## Kafka Guide

`make docker-up` starts Kafka from `docker-compose.yml`, and `make docker-down` stops it.

`make run` publishes one sample `user.created` event to the topic `{{.Slug}}-users` (`KAFKA_TOPIC`), then consumes the topic as the consumer group `{{.Slug}}` (`KAFKA_GROUP_ID`) until Ctrl+C. {{if .Database}}Each `user.created` event becomes a user in {{.Database}}, once per event id, so each run adds exactly one user.{{else}}It logs each event's type, id and size.{{end}} The group resumes after the last message it committed, so a restart doesn't replay the topic. The "Broker workflow" section of `AGENTS.md` describes the event format and the delivery guarantees.

### Watch the topic

```text
$ docker compose exec kafka /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh \
    --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --topic {{.Slug}}-users --from-beginning
```

### Send an event

The worker reads event envelopes; anything else is logged and dropped. The `id` must be a new UUID for each event:

```text
$ docker compose exec kafka /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-producer.sh \
    --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --topic {{.Slug}}-users
>{"id":"7c1f8f9e-8a8e-4a43-9a43-0f9f4b8c2f10","type":"user.created","version":1,"data":{"name":"Ada"}}
```

The worker logs `{{if .Database}}event persisted{{else}}event received{{end}}` with the event's type and id.

### Producer-only and consumer-only workers

Delete the `broker.Consume(...)` call in `cmd/main.go` for a pure producer, or the sample `Publish(...)` for a pure consumer.
